he love avowal is needed to characterize the exact focal expression. The surrounding offer and request for acceptance belong to neighbouring language rather than to the marked words themselves.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"vil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Constance resists Lorck's advances and asks him not to frighten her, Lorck faces her and explicitly declares his love, stressing its seriousness. He then separately offers himself, his life, and his soul and asks whether she will accept them.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no specific undertaking. In the immediately following, separate language, Lorck offers Constance “mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl” and asks her to accept it. The precise practical terms are unspecified, and Constance rejects the offer with “Nej, uf nej.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied evidence explicitly identifies Amalie Skram's novel Constance Ring.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports interpreting Lorck's utterance as a serious avowal and supplies narrative evidence of intense infatuation, but it cannot conclusively establish his sincerity, the durability of his feelings, or concrete terms for his adjacent offer.\"}"
